#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:144
msgid "Resulting in the following generated HTML:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:146
msgid "hello <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">beautiful</span> world!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:152
msgid ""
"The intended use of ``addText`` is larger chunks of plain text, not "
"individual characters.  Custom parsers should buffer plain text output into "
"complete strings rather than sending output one character at a time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:155
msgid "compilerExtensions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:159
msgid ""
"**This is heavily dependent upon compiler internals and may NOT be stable "
"from minor release to minor release.** *It is currently recommended only for "
"1st party grammars.*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:163
msgid ""
"**type**: an array of compiler extensions ie: ``(mode, parentMode) -> {}``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:165
msgid ""
"This allows grammars to extend the mode compiler to add their own syntactic "
"sugar to make reading and writing grammars easier.  The intention is that we "
"use grammars to \"test\" out new compiler extensions and if they perform "
"well promote them into the core library."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:171
msgid "The incoming mode object"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:173
msgid "parentMode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:174
msgid "The parent mode of the mode (null for the top level language mode)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:176
msgid ""
"For example lets look at a tiny well behaved extension to allow us to write "
"``match`` as sugar to better express the intent to \"match a single thing, "
"then end mode\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:182
msgid ""
"compilerExtensions: [\n"
"  (mode, _parentMode) => {\n"
"    // first some quick sanity checks\n"
"    if (!mode.match) return;\n"
"\n"
"    // then check for users doing things that would make no sense\n"
"    if (mode.begin || mode.end) throw new Error(\"begin & end are not "
"supported with match\");\n"
"\n"
"    // copy the match regex into begin\n"
"    mode.begin = mode.match;\n"
"\n"
"    // cleanup: delete our syntactic construct\n"
"    delete mode.match;\n"
"  }\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:198
msgid ""
"Compiler extension functions return nothing. They are expected to mutate the "
"mode itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:204
msgid "Mode Attributes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:207
msgid "className"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:211
msgid "Use ``scope`` instead."
msgstr "請改用 ``scope``。"

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:219 ../../../mode-reference.rst:253
#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:299
msgid "**type**: scope"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:221
msgid ""
"The scope of a given mode. Scopes are converted to CSS class names in HTML "
"markup."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:223
msgid ""
"Multiple modes can have the same scope. This is useful when a language has "
"multiple variants of syntax for one thing like string in single or double "
"quotes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:228
msgid ""
"{\n"
"  scope: \"title.function.call\",\n"
"  begin: /[a-z]+\\(/\n"
"}"
msgstr ""
"{\n"
"  scope: \"title.function.call\",\n"
"  begin: /[a-z]+\\(/\n"
"}"

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:234
msgid ""
"See :doc:`scopes reference</css-classes-reference>` for details on scopes "
"and CSS classes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:237
msgid "begin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:239 ../../../mode-reference.rst:319
msgid "**type**: regexp or array of regexp"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:241
msgid ""
"Regular expression starting a mode. For example a single quote for strings "
"or two forward slashes for C-style comments. If absent, ``begin`` defaults "
"to a regexp that matches anything, so the mode starts immediately."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:244
msgid "This may also be an array.  See :ref:`beginScope`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:249
msgid "beginScope"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:254
msgid "**type**: numeric index of scopes (when ``begin`` is an array)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:256
msgid "This can be used to apply a scope to just the begin match portion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:260
msgid ""
"{\n"
"  begin: /def/,\n"
"  beginScope: \"keyword\"\n"
"}"
msgstr ""
"{\n"
"  begin: /def/,\n"
"  beginScope: \"keyword\"\n"
"}"

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:265
msgid ""
"You can also use ``beginScope`` to individually highlight portions of the "
"match with different scopes by passing an array to ``begin``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:270
msgid ""
"{\n"
"begin: [\n"
"  /function!/,\n"
"  /\\s+/,\n"
"  hljs.IDENT_RE\n"
"],\n"
"beginScope: {\n"
"  1: \"keyword\",\n"
"  3: \"title\"\n"
"},\n"
"}"
msgstr ""
"{\n"
"begin: [\n"
"  /function!/,\n"
"  /\\s+/,\n"
"  hljs.IDENT_RE\n"
"],\n"
"beginScope: {\n"
"  1: \"keyword\",\n"
"  3: \"title\"\n"
"},\n"
"}"

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:282
msgid ""
"This would highlight ``function!`` as a ``keyword`` while highlighting the "
"name of the function as ``title``. The space(s) between would be matched, "
"but not highlighted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:286
msgid ""
"Note: Internally, each regular expression in the array becomes a capture "
"group inside a larger concatenated regex.  If your regular expressions use "
"capture groups (or references) they will be auto-magically renumerated so "
"that they continue to work without any changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:291
msgid ""
"For more info see issue `#3095 <https://github.com/highlightjs/highlight.js/"
"issues/3095>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:295
msgid "endScope"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:300
msgid "**type**: numeric index of scopes (when ``end`` is an array)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:302
msgid ""
"This has the same behavior as ``beginScope`` but applies to the content of "
"the ``end`` match."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:307
msgid ""
"{\n"
"  begin: /FIRST/,\n"
"  end: /LAST/,\n"
"  endScope: \"built_in\"\n"
"}"
msgstr ""
"{\n"
"  begin: /FIRST/,\n"
"  end: /LAST/,\n"
"  endScope: \"built_in\"\n"
"}"

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:315
msgid "match"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:321
msgid ""
"This is simply syntactic sugar for a ``begin`` when no ``end`` expression is "
"necessary.   It may not be used with ``begin`` or ``end`` keys (that would "
"make no sense).  It exists simply to help make grammars more readable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:327
msgid ""
"{\n"
"  scope: \"title\",\n"
"  match: /Fish/\n"
"}"
msgstr ""
"{\n"
"  scope: \"title\",\n"
"  match: /Fish/\n"
"}"

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:332
msgid "This is equivalent to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:336
msgid ""
"{\n"
"  scope: \"title\",\n"
"  begin: /Fish/\n"
"}"
msgstr ""
"{\n"
"  scope: \"title\",\n"
"  begin: /Fish/\n"
"}"

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:343
msgid "on:begin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:345 ../../../mode-reference.rst:375
msgid "**type**: callback (matchData, response)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:347
msgid ""
"This callback is triggered the moment a begin match is detected. "
"``matchData`` includes the typical regex match data; the full match, match "
"groups, etc. The ``response`` object is used to tell the parser how it "
"should handle the match. It can be also used to temporarily store data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:349 ../../../mode-reference.rst:379
msgid ""
"``response.data`` - a simple object data store.  Can be used for building "
"more complex rules where the end rule is dependent on the content of begin, "
"etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:350 ../../../mode-reference.rst:380
msgid ""
"``response.ignoreMatch()`` - pretend as if this match never happened. The "
"mode is not entered. Continues trying subsequent modes in the current mode's "
"``contains`` list"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:352 ../../../mode-reference.rst:382
msgid "For an example of usage see ``END_SAME_AS_BEGIN`` in ``modes.js``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:356
msgid "end"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:358
msgid "**type**: regexp"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:360
msgid ""
"Regular expression ending a mode. For example a single quote for strings or "
"\"$\" (end of line) for one-line comments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:362
msgid ""
"It's often the case that a beginning regular expression defines the entire "
"mode and doesn't need any special ending. For example a number can be "
"defined with ``begin: \"\\\\b\\\\d+\"`` which spans all the digits."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:365
msgid ""
"If absent, ``end`` defaults to a regexp that matches anything, so the mode "
"ends immediately (after possibly matching any ``contains`` sub-modes)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:368
msgid ""
"Sometimes a mode can end not by itself but implicitly with its containing "
"(parent) mode. This is achieved with :ref:`endsWithParent <endsWithParent>` "
"attribute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:373
msgid "on:end"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:377
msgid ""
"This callback is triggered the moment an end match is detected. "
"``matchData`` includes the typical regex match data; the full match, match "
"groups, etc. The ``response`` object is used to tell the parser how it "
"should handle the match. It can also be used to retrieve data stored from a "
"`begin` callback."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:386
msgid "beginKeywords"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:390
msgid ""
"Used instead of ``begin`` for modes starting with keywords to avoid needless "
"repetition:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:394
msgid ""
"{\n"
"  begin: '\\\\b(class|interface)\\\\b',\n"
"  keywords: 'class interface'\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:399
msgid "… can often be shortened to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:403
msgid ""
"{\n"
"  beginKeywords: 'class interface'\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:407
msgid ""
"Unlike the :ref:`keywords <keywords>` attribute, this one allows only a "
"simple list of space separated keywords. If you do need additional features "
"of ``keywords`` or you just need more keywords for this mode you may include "
"``keywords`` along with ``beginKeywords``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:412
msgid ""
"``beginKeywords`` also checks for a ``.`` before or after the keywords and "
"will fail to match if one is found. This is to avoid false positives for "
"method calls or property accesses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:415
msgid ""
"Ex. ``class A { ... }`` would match while ``A.class == B.class`` would not."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:420
msgid "endsWithParent"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:424
msgid "A flag indicating that a mode ends when its parent ends."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:426
msgid ""
"This is best demonstrated by example. In CSS syntax a selector has a set of "
"rules contained within symbols \"{\" and \"}\". Individual rules are "
"separated by \";\" but the last rule may omit the terminating semicolon:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:431
msgid ""
"p {\n"
"  width: 100%;\n"
"  color: red\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:436
msgid ""
"A simple ``end: /;/`` rule is problematic - the parser could get \"stuck\" "
"looking for a ``;`` that it will never find (or find much later) - skipping "
"over valid content that should be highlighted. This is where "
"``endsWithParent`` proves useful:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:442
msgid ""
"{\n"
"  scope: 'rules', begin: /\\{/, end: /\\}/,\n"
"  contains: [\n"
"    {scope: 'rule', /* ... */ end: ';', endsWithParent: true}\n"
"  ]\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:449
msgid ""
"The ``rule`` scope now will end when the parser sees *either* a ``;`` or a "
"``}`` (from the parent)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:454
msgid "endsParent"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:458
msgid ""
"Forces closing of the parent mode right after the current mode is closed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:460
msgid ""
"This is used for modes that don't have an easily expressible ending lexeme "
"but instead could be closed after the last interesting sub-mode is found."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:463
msgid ""
"Here's an example with two ways of defining functions in Elixir, one using a "
"keyword ``do`` and another using a comma:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:468
msgid ""
"def foo :clear, list do\n"
"  :ok\n"
"end\n"
"\n"
"def foo, do: IO.puts \"hello world\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:474
msgid ""
"Note that in the first case the parameter list after the function title may "
"also include a comma. And if we're only interested in highlighting a title "
"we can tell it to end the function definition after itself:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:480
msgid ""
"{\n"
"  scope: 'function',\n"
"  beginKeywords: 'def', end: hljs.MATCH_NOTHING_RE,\n"
"  contains: [\n"
"    {\n"
"      scope: 'title',\n"
"      begin: hljs.IDENT_RE, endsParent: true\n"
"    }\n"
"  ]\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:491
msgid ""
"The ``end: hljs.MATCH_NOTHING_RE`` ensures that function will never end "
"itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:497
msgid "keywords"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:499
msgid "**type**: object / string / array"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:501
msgid "*Keyword definition comes in three forms.*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:503
msgid ""
"A string of space-separated keywords with an optional relevance following a "
"pipe (``|``):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:507
msgid "'for while if|0 else weird_voodoo|10 ...'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:509
msgid "An array of keywords (with optional relevance  following a ``|``):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:513
msgid ""
"[\n"
"  \"for\",\n"
"  \"while\",\n"
"  \"if|0\"\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:521
msgid ""
"It's recommended that the array form be used (one keyword per line) rather "
"than a string to simplify future maintenance. This is the style followed by "
"grammars part of the core library."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:526
msgid ""
"An object that describing multiple sets of keywords and (optionally) the "
"pattern used to locate them:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:531
msgid ""
"{\n"
"  keyword: [ 'for', 'while', 'if|0' ],\n"
"  literal: [ 'true', 'false' ],\n"
"  $pattern: /\\w+/\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:539
msgid ""
"For a more detailed explanation see :doc:`Language definition guide </"
"language-guide>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:543
msgid "illegal"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:545
msgid "**type**: regexp or array"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:547
msgid ""
"A regular expression or array that defines symbols illegal for the mode. "
"When the parser finds an illegal match it may immediately stop parsing the "
"whole language altogether (see ``ignoreIllegals``). Smart use of illegal can "
"greatly improve auto-detection by quickly ruling out a language (when an "
"illegal match is found)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:555
msgid ""
"{\n"
"  illegal: /%/,\n"
"  // or using an array\n"
"  illegal: [ /%/, /cookies/ ]\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:563
msgid "excludeBegin, excludeEnd"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:567
msgid ""
"Excludes beginning or ending matches from a mode's content. For example in "
"CSS syntax a rule ends with a semicolon. However visually it's better not to "
"consider the semicolon as part of the rule's contents. Using ``excludeEnd: "
"true`` forces a ``<span>`` element for the rule to close before the "
"semicolon."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:572
msgid ""
"The semicolon is still consumed by the rule though and cannot be matched by "
"other subsequent rules. (it's effectively been skipped over)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:577
msgid "returnBegin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:581
msgid ""
"Returns just found beginning lexeme back into parser. This is used when "
"beginning of a sub-mode is a complex expression that should not only be "
"found within a parent mode but also parsed according to the rules of a sub-"
"mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:586 ../../../mode-reference.rst:601
msgid ""
"Since the parser is effectively goes back it's quite possible to create a "
"infinite loop here so use with caution! A look-ahead regex is almost always "
"preferable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:591
msgid "returnEnd"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:595
msgid ""
"Returns just found ending lexeme back into parser. This is used for example "
"to parse JavaScript embedded into HTML. A JavaScript block ends with the "
"HTML closing tag ``</script>`` that cannot be parsed with JavaScript rules. "
"So it is returned back into its parent HTML mode that knows what to do with "
"it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:606
msgid "contains"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:608 ../../../mode-reference.rst:627
msgid "**type**: array"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:610
msgid ""
"The list of sub-modes that can be found inside the mode. For detailed "
"explanation see :doc:`Language definition guide </language-guide>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:614
msgid "starts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:616
msgid "**type**: mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:618
msgid ""
"The the mode that will start right after the current mode ends. The new mode "
"will not be contained within the current one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:620
msgid ""
"Currently this attribute is used to highlight JavaScript and CSS contained "
"within HTML. Tags ``<script>`` and ``<style>`` start sub-modes that use "
"another language definition to parse their contents (see :ref:`subLanguage`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:625
msgid "variants"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:629
msgid ""
"Modification to the main definitions of the mode, effectively expanding it "
"into several similar modes each having all the attributes from the main "
"definition augmented or overridden by the variants::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:632
msgid ""
"{\n"
"  scope: 'string',\n"
"  contains: ['self', hljs.BACKSLASH_ESCAPE],\n"
"  relevance: 0,\n"
"  variants: [\n"
"    {begin: /\"/, end: /\"/},\n"
"    {begin: /'/, end: /'/, relevance: 1}\n"
"  ]\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:644
msgid ""
"``variants`` has very specific behavior with regards to ``contains: "
"['self']``. Lets consider the example above. While you might think this "
"would allow you to embed any type of string (double or single quoted) within "
"any other string, **it does not**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:649
msgid "The variants are instead compiled into to two *discrete* modes::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:651
msgid ""
"{ scope: 'string', begin: /\"/, contains: ['self', ... ] }\n"
"{ scope: 'string', begin: /'/, contains: ['self', ... ] }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:654
msgid ""
"Each mode's ``self`` refers only to the new expanded mode, not the original "
"mode with variants (which no longer exists after compiling)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:657
msgid "Further info: https://github.com/highlightjs/highlight.js/issues/826"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:664
msgid "subLanguage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:666
msgid "**type**: string or array"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:668
msgid "Highlights the entire contents of the mode with another language."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:670
msgid ""
"When using this attribute there's no point to define internal parsing rules "
"like :ref:`keywords`, etc. Also it is recommended to avoid the ``scope`` "
"attribute since the sublanguage already wraps the text in its own ``<span "
"class=\"language-name\">`` tag."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:675
msgid ""
"The value of the attribute controls which language or languages will be used "
"for highlighting:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:677
msgid "language name: explicit highlighting with the specified language"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:678
msgid "empty array: auto detection with all the languages available"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:679
msgid ""
"array of language names: auto detection constrained to the specified set"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:683
msgid "skip"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:687
msgid ""
"Skips any markup processing for the mode ensuring that it remains a part of "
"its parent buffer along with the starting and the ending lexemes. This works "
"in conjunction with the parent's :ref:`subLanguage` when it requires complex "
"parsing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:692
msgid "Consider parsing PHP inside HTML:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:694
msgid "<p><? echo 'PHP'; /* ?> */ ?></p>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:698
msgid ""
"The ``?>`` inside the comment should **not** end the PHP part, so we have to "
"handle pairs of ``/* .. */`` to correctly find the ending ``?>``::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:701
msgid ""
"{\n"
"  begin: /<\\?/, end: /\\?>/,\n"
"  subLanguage: 'php',\n"
"  contains: [{begin: '/\\\\*', end: '\\\\*/', skip: true}]\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mode-reference.rst:707
msgid ""
"Without ``skip: true`` every comment would cause the parser to drop out back "
"into the HTML mode."
msgstr ""
